Falling back to `500` is correct for the HTTP response, but an out-of-range status code is still a server-side programming error. We should surface it with a dedicated `FST_ERR_BAD_ERROR_STATUS_CODE`, preserving the original error as its `cause`.

The following is me thinking out loud, I recommend to wait for further review.

We create a new Fastify Internal Error:
```js
FST_ERR_BAD_ERROR_STATUS_CODE: createError(
  'FST_ERR_BAD_ERROR_STATUS_CODE',
  'Invalid status code on Error: %s'
)
```

We update the signature of `setErrorStatusCode`
```js
function setErrorStatusCode (reply, err) {
  if (!reply[kReplyHasStatusCode] || reply.statusCode === 200) {
    const statusCode = err && (err.statusCode || err.status)

    if (statusCode !== undefined && !isValidErrorStatusCode(statusCode)) {
      reply.code(500)
      // err is replaced by `FST_ERR_BAD_ERROR_STATUS_CODE` keeping the orginal
     // error reference.
      return new FST_ERR_BAD_ERROR_STATUS_CODE(statusCode, {
        cause: err
      })
    }

    reply.code(statusCode || 500)
  }

  return err
}
```

We then can use it for `diagnostics` so monitoring tools can catch it.:
```js
// Set status code before publishing so subscribers see the correct value
const withErrorWrongStatus = setErrorStatusCode(reply, err)
channels.error.publish(withErrorWrongStatus)
```

See: https://github.com/fastify/fastify/blob/d266f833f2bff34c6115c026e461b682e94d0b9c/lib/handle-request.js#L193

This can hurt performance as it sits on the hot path, can you just verify against `nullish` values instead?


```suggestion
  let cachedFns = this[kRouteContext][kReplyCacheSerializeFns]?.get(schema)
  if (cachedFns != null && cachedFns.has(cacheKey)) {
    return cachedFns.get(cacheKey)
```

Instead of relying human fixed value, I would battle test the result between `node:util`.

```js
const { MIMEType } = require('node:util')

test('application/json; charset=utf-8,application/json', (t) => {
      t.plan(4)
      const ct = new ContentType('application/json; charset=utf-8,application/json')
      const mt = new MIMEType('application/json; charset=utf-8,application/json')
      t.assert.strictEqual(ct.type, mt.type)
      t.assert.strictEqual(ct.subtype, mt.subtype)
      t.assert.strictEqual(ct.mediaType, mt.essence)
      t.assert.strictEqual(String(ct), String(mt))
})

**[AI: MiniMax M3]** The size guard has a small race window. If `payload.destroy(error)` schedules an `error` event in the same tick the underlying socket flushes `end`, both `done(error)` (from the error listener) and `done(null, stream)` (from the end listener) can fire. Add a `destroyed` flag and short-circuit both handlers:

```js
let destroyed = false
payload.on('data', (chunk) => {
  if (destroyed) return
  size += chunk.length
  if (size > limit) {
    destroyed = true
    payload.destroy(Object.assign(new Error('Request body is too large'), { statusCode: 413 }))
    return
  }
  chunks.push(chunk)
})
payload.on('end', () => {
  if (destroyed) return
  ...
})
payload.on('error', (err) => {
  if (destroyed) return
  done(err)
})
```

